      "source": [
        "#1.Gathering data via API : dataset collection and curation\n",
        "\n",
        "The first thing to do is to gather data from the English Wikipedia API. To do so, we will go through a complete data extraction procedure, using classical request functions. In particular, we will use the python library `requests`. The goal of this first part is to build a dataset of movie articles.\n",
        "\n",
        "As required, we will follow specific rules to avoid over-\n",
        "loading Wikipedia’s servers."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "1W5WwtPuSNBv"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 1.1 Get the articles in the category"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "HUD2zHEBVVmt"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# We first define a User-Agent header to identify the requests to the API\n",
        "headers = {\n",
        "    'User-Agent': 'ENSAI-NLP-Lab/1.0 (thomas.goumont@eleve.ensai.fr)'\n",
        "}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "After discussion, we (Léo and Thomas) decided to select the category of movies for the year 2023, as we both went a lot to the cinema during this specific year. It is then easier for us to identify which movies we will be talking about.\n",
        "\n",
        "The first task is then to collect the article titles for the films released in 2023. Then, we will construct the following method: it will take as input a specific film category (*e.g.* the \"2023 films\" category), go through the MediaWiki API and get all the members of this category (*i.e.* all the films released in 2023). It will allow us to obtain the elements that will constitute our corpus of documents for our text analysis. Each document will be one of the element of the \"2O23 films\" category, and the corpus that will be studied is then the set of all the documents.\n",
        "\n",
        "The resulting method, `get_category_members`, is defined below. It consists in a `GET` request, with the 'categorymembers' module in order to list the pages that belong to the \"2023 films\" category. It extracts a significant sample of 500 elements, without over-loading the server thanks to `time.sleep(0.5)`. The requested data are then converted into a .json file, because the Wikipedia API returns initially hierarchical data, which JSON files support naturally, rather than .csv file that is flat/tabular."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "0qkAjN_0vndq"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "RLPTE8rxvFjC"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "def get_category_members(category, limit=500):   # We set the maximum results per query at 500, as suggested\n",
        "    \"\"\" Request the elements of a chosen category from the English Wikipedia API.\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    base_url = \"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php\" # The API endpoint\n",
        "    params = {                                   # Those are the parameters of our request: it will ask\n",
        "                                                 # the API to return the list of all elements of the selected category\n",
        "        'action': 'query',\n",
        "        'list': 'categorymembers',\n",
        "        'cmtitle': f'Category:{category}',\n",
        "        'cmlimit': limit,\n",
        "        'format': 'json'\n",
        "    }\n",
        "\n",
        "    try:\n",
        "\n",
        "      response = requests.get(base_url, headers=headers, params=params) # It sends the code of the GET request\n",
        "      time.sleep(0.5) # We set a delay each request, to avoid over-loading the server\n",
        "      response.raise_for_status()  # This will raise an exception for HTTP errors that can occur\n",
        "      data = response.json() # We store the results and convert them into a .json dictionary,\n",
        "                             # easier to handle than .csv\n",
        "\n",
        "      members = data.get('query', {}).get('categorymembers', [])\n",
        "\n",
        "      titles = [member['title'] for member in members]\n",
        "\n",
        "      return titles\n",
        "    except requests.exceptions.RequestException as e:\n",
        "      print(f\"Error API: {e}\")\n",
        "      return []"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Therefore, the first 500 entries constitute a sufficiently representative sample for the subsequent text mining tasks.\n",
        "\n",
        "A question that we had was regarding the way that we requested the films. Indeed, the above method takes the \"first\" 500 movies, *i.e.* the movies in the alphabetical order, so we were thinking about potential sampling bias. For example, imagine that, for a specific year, 300 movies began with the letter A, it will lead to an imbalanced dataset with much more movies beginning with A. However, we finally thought that, although the API returns titles in lexicographical order, we believe this does not introduce a semantic bias in our NLP pipeline, and that we don't need to \"shuffle\" (*i.e.* taking results from a random mix of the movie's first letter) the requested films, in particular because the initial letter of a movie title is stochastically independent of its plot, genre (*e.g.* Alpha and Anaconda, both released in 2025), or linguistic structure. We then decided to keep our method as defined above.\n",
        "\n",
        "The method is then used to obtain the elements that belong to our chosen category."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "5r9-bLpZSRyU"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
