egory:Cinematic and Box Office Achievement Golden Globe winners', 'Category:Cultural depictions of American people', 'Category:Cultural depictions of Bill Clinton', 'Category:Cultural depictions of Ronald Reagan', 'Category:Dolby Cinema films', 'Category:English-language adventure comedy films', 'Category:English-language fantasy adventure films', 'Category:English-language fantasy comedy films', 'Category:Existentialist films', 'Category:Fictional matriarchies', 'Category:Films about horses', 'Category:Films about misogyny', 'Category:Films about mother–daughter relationships', 'Category:Films about sentient toys', 'Category:Films based on Mattel toys', 'Category:Films directed by Greta Gerwig', 'Category:Films impacted by the COVID-19 pandemic', 'Category:Films produced by David Heyman', 'Category:Films produced by Margot Robbie', 'Category:Films produced by Robbie Brenner', 'Category:Films produced by Tom Ackerley', 'Category:Films set in Los Angeles', 'Category:Films set on beaches', 'Category:Films shot at Warner Bros. Studios Leavesden', 'Category:Films shot in Los Angeles', 'Category:Films that won the Best Original Song Academy Award', 'Category:Films with screenplays by Greta Gerwig', 'Category:Films with screenplays by Noah Baumbach', 'Category:Heyday Films films', 'Category:IMDb ID (Cite Mojo) different from Wikidata', 'Category:Internet memes introduced in 2023', 'Category:LGBTQ-related controversies in film', 'Category:LuckyChap Entertainment films', 'Category:Metafictional works', \"Category:Nickelodeon Kids' Choice Award–winning films\", 'Category:Pages using multiple image with auto scaled images', 'Category:Postmodern films', 'Category:Satellite Award–winning films', 'Category:Saturn Award–winning films', 'Category:Short description is different from Wikidata', 'Category:Template film date with 2 release dates', 'Category:Use American English from August 2025', 'Category:Use mdy dates from July 2023', 'Category:Warner Bros. films']\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Then, we complete the second method, `get_movie_genres`, which serves as the processing layer. It performs a keyword spotting analysis, by converting category titles to lowercase and matching them against our pre-defined keywords-genre dictionary. It allows us to map complex strings (*e.g.* \"Category:2023 French romantic comedy films\") to standardized labels (\"romance\", \"comedy\")."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ItnxNX9UeeWt"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "mvJB6EIHhTlr"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "def get_movie_genres(title):\n",
        "  \"\"\" Request the genres of a chosen page from the English Wikipedia API.\n",
        "  \"\"\"\n",
        "  categories = get_page_categories(title) # First, we retrieve all the raw categories of the page,\n",
        "                                            # using the above method\n",
        "  detected_genres = set()\n",
        "\n",
        "  for cat in categories: # We seek if there is a keywords in the categorie's name belongs to\n",
        "                         # the above dictionary. For example, the word \"comedy\" is in\n",
        "                         # \"Category: American comedy films\"\n",
        "      cat_lower = cat.lower()\n",
        "\n",
        "      for genre in genre_keywords:\n",
        "          if genre.lower() in cat_lower:\n",
        "              if genre.lower() == \"science fiction\": # Normalisation for th science-fiction genre\n",
        "                  detected_genres.add(\"Sci-Fi\")\n",
        "              else:\n",
        "                  detected_genres.add(genre)\n",
        "\n",
        "  return sorted(list(detected_genres)) # Returns the retrieved list of genre for the movies,\n",
        "                                       # which is sorted for consistency"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# An example of the second method\n",
        "\n",
        "print(get_movie_genres(\"Barbie (film)\"))"
