            "['adventure', 'comedy', 'epic', 'fantasy', 'war']\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "In our NLP pipeline, the quality of the labels depends on the granularity of the categories. A key advantage of the above method is that it allows for multi-label classification: a movie like Barbie can be simultaneously tagged as \"comedy\", \"fantasy\", and \"adventure\". This reflects the real-world complexity of film genres more accurately than a single-label system.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, we are still aware that this method relies on the proxy assumption: we indeed assume before that if a keyword appears in a category title, it is a primary characteristic of the film.\n",
        "\n",
        "Another observation can be made regarding this method. Indeed, because there is no exhaustive categories, a category named \"Films about war crimes\" could tag a film both as a \"war\" movie and \"crime\" one, whereas it is a trial documentary. Therefore, this method could possibly capture *false positives* during the process, and we need to be aware of this limitation."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Lfvj9J8bfCDP"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "3AXIdbNThhml"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 1.3 Creating the corpus: Data orchestration and persistence\n",
        "\n",
        "We now go through the final step of the collection process, by gathering the whole corpus that will be used during this project, using the functions defined previously into a single extraction pipeline. The goal is to transform a simple list of Wikipedia titles into a structured dataset, directly operational for an NLP pipeline.\n",
        "\n",
        "First, for each movie, we perform a sequential retrieval of its clean textual content and its proxy-genres. By encapsulating this in a try/except block, we ensure that a single network error or a malformed page does not interrupt the entire collection process. To represent our corpus, we choose a list of dictionaries, where each object contains the title, the textual body (the feature for NLP), and the genres (seen later as labels for clustering). This JSON format is ideal for subsequent tasks as it preserves the relationship between the text and its metadata.\n",
        "\n",
        "Finally, the corpus is exported as a .json file using UTF-8 encoding to preserve special characters. Downloading the file locally is a necessary step to ensure reproducibility, as the Google Colab environment is ephemeral.\n",
        "\n",
        "The final procedure is given by the code below."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "9ucBMvUuzEH1"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "def gather_corpus_from_category(category, limit=10):\n",
        "  \"\"\" Gather a corpus of documents from a chosen category.\n",
        "  \"\"\"\n",
        "  titles = get_category_members(category, limit) # We use the first method to get the list of page titles\n",
        "  corpus = []                                    # in the \"2023 films\" category\n",
        "\n",
        "  for i, title in enumerate(titles): # We perform a loop on each title to retrieve the content for each\n",
        "      try:\n",
        "          content = get_page_content_plaintxt(title) # Here, we use the second method (\"plaintexte\")\n",
        "                                                       # to retrieve the clean text\n",
        "          genres = get_movie_genres(title) # Then, we retrieve the genres using the previous method\n",
        "\n",
        "          if content: # If we indeed find content, we add it to the corpus,\n",
        "                      # following a specific json structure\n",
        "              article_data = {\n",
        "                    'title': title,\n",
        "                    'content': content,\n",
        "                    'genres': genres\n",
        "                }\n",
        "              corpus.append(article_data)\n",
        "\n",
        "          print(f\"[{i+1}/{len(titles)}] Done: {title}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "      except Exception as e:\n",
        "          print(f\"Error occured for {title}: {e}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "      time.sleep(0.5) # We again keep a time step between requests to the API,\n",
        "                      # to avoid over-loading Wikipedia's server\n",
        "  return corpus"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "We can notice that we adopted a conservative rate-limiting strategy, by keeping `time.sleep(0.5)` in the loop, and each method `get_page_content_plaintxt` and `get_movie_genres` make a request to the API, so, for each movie, we wait 0.5s + the response time of the two requests. This ensures that the server is not overloaded by our requests.\n",
        "\n",
        "Also, including the condition `if content` allows us to keep only articles for which we indeed have content. This is done to ensure the quality of the corpus, especially for the clustering phase, to avoid the pages with errors or redirections."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "AimkMlhckQrW"
      }
    },
