    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "We successfully retrieved and cleaned 499 valid movie articles (one article was filtered out due to empty content or retrieval error). The exploratory analysis reveals different characteristics of our corpus that will influence our NLP pipeline.\n",
        "\n",
        "First, initial descriptive statistics reveal a significant variance in article length: while the majority of pages contain between 200 and 1,500 words, meaning that they are concise descriptions, we observe a **long-tail distribution** (heavily right-skewed), with a few highly documented films exceeding 5,000 words. We can suspect these outliers to be major blockbusters, which generate more extensive documentation on Wikipedia. Nevertheless, this high variance suggests that simple frequency-based vectorizations (such as CountVectorizer) might be biased towards long documents. We will likely need normalization techniques or length-penalties during the clustering phase to ensure fair comparisons between shortly descripted films and major productions.\n",
        "\n",
        "Second, regarding the distribution of categories per page, the barplot for genre analysis confirms that drama, comedy, and thriller are the dominant categories for the year 2023, while genres like western or disaster are rare. It is also interesting to note the high frequency of documentary and horror films, which often follow very specific lexical patterns. We can say that multi-label nature of our dataset is evident here, with movies assigned to different genres. This provides a rich basis for our upcoming spectral clustering task (see section 6), where we will evaluate if textual representations can successfully reconstruct these human-labeled categories.\n",
        "\n",
        "We also notice that the genre distribution is significantly imbalanced. The drama category is by far dominant, acting as a \"super-genre\" that encompasses many other types of films. This imbalance poses a challenge for clustering, because the algorithms might struggle to separate distinct sub-genres from the dominant drama cluster. We will need to see if the spectral clustering can capture semantic nuances beyond these broad labels.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "GSxpSQcZawNB"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`6.` It is also important to analyze the linguistic characteristics of our corpus, as it will provide insights about the retrieved data that can help us to go through the NLP pipeline.\n",
        "\n",
        "We can first look at the previous python cell, in which we computed the type-token ratio (TTR), which gives us the lexical wealth of our corpus, and enables us to assess the linguistic complexity of our dataset as well. The distribution, centered around 0.55 (the mean TTR), indicates a relatively rich vocabulary, typical of edited encyclopedic content (*i.e.* roughly 55% of the words in a document are unique). The boxplot shows a consistent distribution, but we notice that shorter articles tend to have an artificially higher TTR. This situation refers to a known bias in linguistics (partially explained by the fact that longer articles naturally repeat vocabulary) [3], which we must account for during the clustering phase.\n",
        "\n",
        "Again, by looking at the results of the previous cell, we see that the global average sentence length stands at 21.93 words per sentence. This result is significantly higher than conversational English (typically 10-15 words). It can be explained by the fact that length is characteristic of encyclopedic writing, where contributors frequently use compound-complex sentences and embedded clauses (appositions, relative clauses) to condense detailed plot summaries and production facts into dense paragraphs. For our NLP pipeline, this implies a higher dependency parsing complexity, as the syntactic distance between subjects and their associated verbs or objects is likely to be substantial."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "J6lmSxUiZS3g"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "To deepen our structural analysis, we performed Part-of-Speech (POS) tagging using spaCy's statistical models. This process involves assigning a grammatical category (such as noun, verb, adjective) to each token based on its definition and context. POS tagging allows us to capture the syntactic nature of the text. We specifically monitored the distribution of proper nouns (PROPN), nouns, verbs, and adjectives, as these categories carry the most semantic weight in information extraction tasks.\n",
        "\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "YtjsiW3tl6DD"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "def get_pos_distribution(text):\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    Analyzes the grammatical structure of the text using spaCy.\n",
        "    Returns the proportion of Nouns, Verbs, Adjectives, and Proper Nouns.\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    doc = nlp(text) # We use the NLP processing pipeline (tokenization, tagging, parsing)\n",
        "\n",
        "    # We filter out punctuation and whitespace to focus on semantic content\n",
        "    tokens = [token for token in doc if not token.is_punct and not token.is_space]\n",
        "\n",
        "    num_tokens = len(tokens)\n",
        "\n",
        "    # We check if an article is empty or have only punctuation, and then return 0\n",
        "    if num_tokens == 0:\n",
        "        return {'NOUN': 0, 'VERB': 0, 'ADJ': 0, 'PROPN': 0}\n",
        "\n",
        "    # We will use .pos_ attribute to get the coarse-grained part-of-speech tag,\n",
        "    # and use a counter for computational efficiency\n",
        "    pos_counts = Counter([token.pos_ for token in tokens])\n",
        "\n",
        "    # We perform normalization to handle varying article lengths, by dividing by the total token count,\n",
        "    # which prevents longer articles from skewing the statistics purely by volume\n",
        "    return {\n",
        "        'NOUN': pos_counts.get('NOUN', 0) / num_tokens,\n",
        "        'VERB': pos_counts.get('VERB', 0) / num_tokens,\n",
        "        'ADJ': pos_counts.get('ADJ', 0) / num_tokens,\n",
        "        'PROPN': pos_counts.get('PROPN', 0) / num_tokens\n",
        "    }\n",
        "\n",
        "# Then, we apply the function to the corpus\n",
        "df_pos = df['content'].apply(get_pos_distribution).apply(pd.Series)\n",
        "\n",
        "# We oncatenate only the new columns (the one with POS tags) to the existing dataframe\n",
        "df = pd.concat([df, df_pos], axis=1)\n",
        "print(df[['title', 'NOUN', 'VERB', 'ADJ', 'PROPN']].head())\n",
        "\n",
        "# We visualize the results using a simple barplot\n",
        "sns.set_theme(style=\"whitegrid\")\n",
        "plt.rcParams['font.family'] = 'sans-serif'\n",
        "\n",
        "pos_cols = ['NOUN', 'VERB', 'ADJ', 'PROPN']\n",
        "mean_pos = df[pos_cols].mean().sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
        "ax = sns.barplot(x=mean_pos.index, y=mean_pos.values, palette=\"viridis\", hue=mean_pos.index, legend=False)\n",
        "\n",
        "for i, v in enumerate(mean_pos.values):\n",
        "    ax.text(i, v + 0.005, f\"{v:.1%}\", ha='center', va='bottom', fontweight='bold', fontsize=12)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.title('Average Part-of-Speech Distribution (Normalized)', fontsize=15, fontweight='bold', pad=20)\n",
        "plt.ylabel('Proportion of Tokens', fontsize=12)\n",
        "plt.xlabel('Grammatical Category', fontsize=12)\n",
        "plt.ylim(0, max(mean_pos.values) + 0.05)\n",
        "sns.despine(left=True)\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
