    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The analysis of the POS distribution reveals a specific \"encyclopedic\" form for movie articles. The most striking feature is the proper nouns (PROPN ~24.6%), which represent nearly a quarter of the text, surpassing common nouns. This is explained by the nature of the corpus: movie summaries are densely packed with named entities, such as actors, characters, or directors.\n",
        "\n",
        "Furthermore, the ratio of nouns to verbs (NOUN ~17.7% against VERB ~10.3%) indicates a style that is more static and descriptive than narrative. Wikipedia articles focus on defining objects (*e.g.* plot or cast) rather than relating actions in a literary style.\n",
        "\n",
        "Finally, the relatively low proportion of adjectives (ADJ ~5.5%) likely reflects Wikipedia's neutral policy, where contributors tend to avoid subjective descriptors in favor of factual statements, resulting in a cleaner (maybe drier) text."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "_wARYFH1jgVS"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`7.`  After this quick look toward basic statistics of our corpus, we are now able to perform a critical analysis regarding the dataset, to highlight its strengths and limitations.\n",
        "\n",
        "First of all, our corpus is defined by its high semantic density and semi-structured format. Its primary strength lies in the combination of rich metadata (with the \"categories\"), with high-quality encyclopedic text. This structure provides a robust ground truth for NLP tasks. The heavy use of named entities makes it an ideal playground for information extraction (see section 3 about Named entity recognition), while the multi-label genre classification offers a basis for spectral clustering. The text is grammatically normative, reducing the noise typically found in social media datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, our data analysis shows that the corpus exhibits some biases that must be addressed. For example, blockbusters are over-represented with extensive summaries, while independent films often have concise descriptions, which can be interpreted as a \"popularity bias\". This risks causing clustering algorithms to group films based on document length rather than semantic content.\n",
        "Furthermore, using user-generated categories as a proxy for genre may introduce ambiguity.\n",
        "Even if we restricted ourselves to the guidelines of this project, we can still say that restricting the scope of our corpus to a single year can introduce temporal bias, potentially overfitting the model to specific cultural trends of that year.\n",
        "\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "fMHlx-ivlxUu"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`8.` To overcome the limitations of Wikipedia's unstructured metadata, we could adopt an enrichment strategy by integrating structured data from domain-specific databases. For example, The Movie Database (TMDb) [4] would provide standardized features (such as budget or box office) and verified genre tags to replace our noisy proxy labels. It would however require appropriate web-scraping algorithms, that may be time consuming. Integrating cinematic reviews would also add a sentimental dimension, allowing us to correlate linguistic complexity with critical reception from the audience, that is not really present in Wikipedia's article, due to the neutral policy of the platform.\n",
        "\n",
        "Regarding tools and libraries that could be used, we\n",
        "can mention [BeautifulSoup](https://beautiful-soup-4.readthedocs.io/en/latest/) or [Scrapy](https://www.scrapy.org/), which could be used to scrape review aggregators that lack public APIs. Then, to merge external data with our Wikipedia titles, a string matching library like [fuzzywuzzy](https://pypi.org/project/fuzzywuzzy/) (which is based on [Levenshtein distance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Levenshtein_distance)) would be crucial for handling title inconsistencies (*e.g.* in order to match \"Barbie\" with \"Barbie (2023 film)\").\n",
        "\n",
        "For review analysis, we could perform feature engineering by employing Hugging Face Transformers (*e.g.*, the [distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english](https://huggingface.co/distilbert/distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english) classifier, which seems to be a powerful tool for text classification) to extract high-level sentiment embeddings, enriching the document vectors. We could perform \"sentiment scoring\", by running scraped reviews through the model, and then extract a probability score. We could therefore append this as a new feature column to our dataset. Alternatively, we could extract the model's dense vector embeddings (*i.e.* the hidden states) to capture the emotional style of the reviews. We could then compute a cosine similarity between these sentiment vectors to cluster movies not just by their plot content, but by how they were perceived by the audience (*e.g.* grouping \"polarizing\" movies together). More precisely, the sentiment vector from movie A is compared with the one of movie B, and if we get a high score, it means that the audience perceived the films with similar emotions."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "hTbHQgiOuzNg"
      }
    },
