    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "LK1XJBxnZra-"
      },
      "source": [
        "#3.Named entity recognition: performing information extraction from the corpus data\n",
        "\n",
        "In NLP, extracting specific information from text data is a common task, that can be applied to a wide range of information's type (identifying names, semantic relationships, or detecting events) [5]. In this project, we will focus on Named entity recognition (NER), by extracting named entities from our movie articles and analyzing their co-occurences."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "spr6kDIEWgyg"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 3.1. IOB tagging"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "29XRhHlRWjKn"
      },
      "source": [
        "`9.` As seen during the lectures [1], the Inside-Outside-Beginning (IOB) tagging scheme is a standard sequence labelling format for named entity annotation. It is used to mark both the type of an entity and its position in the text. More precisely, each token is labelled O if it does not belong to any entity, or B-XXX / I-XXX if it marks respectively the beginning or the inside of an entity of type XXX (PERSON, ORG, etc.). This makes it possible to represent explicit entity boundaries, including multi-word mentions, and to avoid merging two adjacent entities of the same type.\n",
        "\n",
        "In practice, NER is formulated as a sequence tagging problem, where the model predicts an IOB tag for each token in a sentence. Classical approaches rely on Conditional Random Fields (CRFs), which exploit dependencies between neighbouring labels and hand-crafted linguistic features (*e.g.* word shape, POS tags, local context). Also, more recent neural methods, such as BiLSTM-CRF architectures or Transformer-based models (like the spaCy pipeline used here), learn contextual representations automatically and generally achieve more robust and accurate entity detection."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "cQdfGi-UWrjP"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 3.2. NER extraction\n",
        "\n",
        "`10.` To extract named entities from our corpus, we use the spaCy `en_core_web_md` model. As requested, we prioritized computational efficiency by customizing the pipeline.\n",
        "\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "VVL3S6GSWrFy",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "outputId": "3ae3ca22-f5a5-44cf-ea97-eb69e91504b7"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "[('3', 'CARDINAL'), ('2023', 'CARDINAL'), ('Indian', 'NORP'), ('Rajesh Sharma', 'PERSON'), ('Chetan Daiya', 'PERSON')]\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# We first reload the model strictly for NER tasks\n",
        "\n",
        "nlp = spacy.load(\n",
        "    \"en_core_web_md\",\n",
        "    disable=[\"tagger\", \"parser\", \"lemmatizer\", \"attribute_ruler\"] # Here, we disable 'tagger', 'parser',\n",
        ")                                                                 # and 'lemmatizer', because these\n",
        "                                                                  # components are computationally expensive\n",
        "                                                                  # and unnecessary for pure NER, so disabling\n",
        "                                                                  # them reduces time cost\n",
        "\n",
        "def extract_entities_batch(texts, batch_size=50):\n",
        "    \"\"\" Extracts named entities using spaCy's pipe method for efficient batch processing.\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    entities_list = []\n",
        "\n",
        "    for doc in nlp.pipe(texts, batch_size=batch_size): # We use nlp.pipe, as it processes texts as a batch,\n",
        "                                                       # which is much faster than applying the model to each\n",
        "                                                       # row of the dataset\n",
        "        ents = [(ent.text, ent.label_) for ent in doc.ents]  # We store entities as tuples (text, label)\n",
        "        entities_list.append(ents)\n",
        "\n",
        "    return entities_list\n",
        "\n",
        "# We apply the batch extraction to the whole content column at once\n",
        "df[\"entities\"] = extract_entities_batch(df[\"content\"])\n",
        "print(df[\"entities\"].iloc[0][:5]) # We print the first 5 entities of the first movie"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Here, we performed a quick pipeline optimization. Indeed, we know that the standard spaCy pipeline includes a POS tagger, a dependency parser, and a lemmatizer, but our current objective is only about NER, so we explicitly disabled these downstream components. It accelerates the inference time, as the model skips the syntactic parsing steps.\n",
        "Furthermore, instead of processing documents one by one, we used the `nlp.pipe()` method, as it allows us to perform batch processing, enabling the model to treat multiple documents in parallel.\n",
        "\n",
        "The results are then stored as a list of tuples (entity text, entity label) within the dataframe."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "zeTaR-k27HvQ"
      }
    },
