      ],
      "source": [
        "# We then print the top 20 entities, for each type of entity\n",
        "\n",
        "all_entities = df[\"entities\"].explode().dropna()\n",
        "df_entities = pd.DataFrame(all_entities.tolist(), columns=['text', 'label'])\n",
        "\n",
        "unique_labels = df_entities['label'].unique() # We get the list of each unique label of entities\n",
        "\n",
        "for label in unique_labels: # We print the top 20 for each of them\n",
        "    print(f\"\\n Top 20 most frequent entities for: {label}\")\n",
        "    print(df_entities[df_entities['label'] == label]['text'].value_counts().head(20))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "By looking at the distribution of entities per type, we can derive precise insights about our corpus composition.\n",
        "\n",
        "We first notice a dominance of noisy information, represented by the CARDINAL and DATE lists, which are the most populated. Terms like \"2023\" (1256 occurrences), \"2022\", and also spelled-out numbers (\"one\", or \"two\") dominate these entities. We indeed consider that numerical entities represent noise inherent to the encyclopedic format (that contains release dates of movies or box office rankings), but offer little semantic value of a movie.\n",
        "\n",
        "Regarding the content of the corpus, the top entity of the PERSON entity's type is \"Barbie\" (191), which aligns perfectly with the cinematic trends of 2023.\n",
        "For the NORP entity's type, \"Indian\" (209) appears more frequently than \"American\" (146), and \"The Times of India\" is the second most cited organization. This suggests a strong representation of Bollywood or Tollywood cinema in our sample, potentially due to the alphabetical sampling or the high volume of Indian film pages created on Wikipedia. For the ORG's type, besides production giants like \"Warner Bros.\" and \"Netflix\", we see frequent mentions of media outlets (*e.g.* \"Guardian\"), confirming that Wikipedia articles often cite press reviews.\n",
        "\n",
        "At the same time, we can highlight some limitations for the model. Indeed, some labelling errors appear, for instance “Rotten Tomatoes” (a well known movie's review website) is tagged as PERSON instead of ORG, which illustrates the limits of a generic NER model on a domain-specific corpus centered on movies.  Beyond this error, we observe other hallucinations. For example, \"Metacritic\" appears as a WORK_OF_ART, and the word \"Tomatoes\" is curiously classified as MONEY. These errors confirm that the generic `en_core_web_md` model struggles with the specific jargon of film.\n",
        "\n",
        "Hence, this distribution of our corpus confirms that raw entity extraction is noisy. Therefore, for downstream analyses (in particular, for co-occurrences and entity-based distances), filtering out uninformative types (*e.g.* CARDINAL, ORDINAL, or even DATE and PERCENT) and correcting or regrouping certain entities (media,platforms) would help emphasise entities that are actually informative for characterising films (such as directors, actors, countries, studios, etc.). We should also be cautious with PERSON and ORG entities, due to the observed misclassifications of review websites.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "NnIaTGev9qDv"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`12.` The following function will count the number of co-occurrences of two\n",
        "entities within the same document in the corpus, by taking as inputs a pair of entities and their corresponding types."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ullReay_BzbS"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "I3nM3PE8W82d",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "outputId": "b4fa09d4-808c-4235-bf66-81f8a3d0a820"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Co-occurrences of 'Barbie' (PERSON) and 'American' (NORP): 4\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# We define the following method to count the co-occurences\n",
        "\n",
        "def count_cooccurrences(entity1, type1, entity2, type2, df):\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    Counts the number of documents where two specific entities appear together.\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    cooccurrence_count = 0\n",
        "\n",
        "    for doc_entities in df[\"entities\"]:\n",
        "        # We convert the list to a set to improve the speed of the method\n",
        "        entity_set = set(doc_entities) # doc_entities is already a list of (text, label) tuples\n",
        "\n",
        "        # We check if both entities are present in the document's set\n",
        "        if (entity1, type1) in entity_set and (entity2, type2) in entity_set:\n",
        "            cooccurrence_count += 1\n",
        "\n",
        "    return cooccurrence_count\n",
        "\n",
        "# We can test the method with the most frequent \"PERSON\" and \"GPE\" found earlier\n",
        "count = count_cooccurrences(\"Barbie\", \"PERSON\", \"American\", \"NORP\", df)\n",
        "print(f\"Co-occurrences of 'Barbie' (PERSON) and 'American' (NORP): {count}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "We define co-occurrence here at the document level. This means we count the number of documents where both entity A and entity B appear at least once. This is then a binary approach, *i.e.* whether the entities appear together once or three times in the same text, it counts as a single co-occurrence event for that document.  Also, in the implementation, we decided to convert the list of entities for each document into a Python set, to reduce the complexity."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "egsgc__1rnSz"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`13.` Using the previous method defined in question `12.`, we can then print the pairs of entities\n",
        "of different types that most frequently appear together. We set the number of most frequent pairs printed to 10, as it seems for us to be sufficient to analyze the results.\n",
        "\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "L2OzrkvuD5aG"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "aj-tTe5wXL8V",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "outputId": "d548a64c-f2c6-4b5f-f97d-4d9ae255db02"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "2023 (CARDINAL) - first (ORDINAL) : 181\n",
            "2023 (CARDINAL) - Indian (NORP) : 149\n",
            "2023 (CARDINAL) - American (NORP) : 119\n",
            "2023 (CARDINAL) - Rotten Tomatoes (PERSON) : 114\n",
            "2023 (CARDINAL) - second (ORDINAL) : 77\n",
            "2023 (CARDINAL) - the United States (GPE) : 72\n",
            "2023 (CARDINAL) - The Times of India (ORG) : 70\n",
            "Indian (NORP) - The Times of India (ORG) : 70\n",
            "first (ORDINAL) - two (CARDINAL) : 70\n",
            "5 (CARDINAL) - Indian (NORP) : 69\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# We use a counter to show the most fequent pairs of entities that appear in our corpus\n",
        "\n",
        "pair_counter = Counter()\n",
        "\n",
        "for entities in df[\"entities\"]:\n",
        "    for (e1, t1), (e2, t2) in combinations(set(entities), 2):\n",
        "        if t1 != t2:\n",
        "            pair = tuple(sorted([(e1, t1), (e2, t2)]))\n",
        "            pair_counter[pair] += 1\n",
        "\n",
        "top_pairs = pair_counter.most_common(10)\n",
        "for ((e1, t1), (e2, t2)), c in top_pairs:\n",
        "    print(f\"{e1} ({t1}) - {e2} ({t2}) : {c}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The most frequent pairs almost always involve the year \"2023\" (CARDINAL) associated with other very frequent entities such as \"first\", \"second\", \"Indian\", \"American\", \"Rotten Tomatoes\", \"The Times of India\", \"the United States\", and so on. These co‑occurrences mainly reflect recurrent phrasing in film articles (*e.g.* \"2023 Indian film [...]\", or \"In 2023, Rotten Tomatoes reported that [...]\") rather than specific  relationships between two entities (*e.g.* between a particular actor and a particular studio). In other words, during this information extraction task, we mostly capture editorial patterns of Wikipedia, not meaningful semantic links at the level of individual films.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, according to our analysis, one of the pairs of co-occurences stands out: the \"Indian\" and \"The Times of India\" relation, with 69 co-occurrences. Unlike the generic date associations that appear a lot in our corpus, this co-occurrence reflects a semantic link, because indian films are heavily referenced by their local press. This reinforces our previous observation (see question `11.`) regarding the strong representation of Bollywood cinema in our sample."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "kTzWNIdjXZ0h"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`14.` Taking into account our analysis for the previous question, we can say that analysing co-occurrences at the full-document level is of limited relevance. Indeed, the top co-occurrences are dominated by structural noise, capturing the encyclopedic format of the articles rather than the content of the films, and entities may co-occur simply because they are frequent in this type of article, or because they appear in very distant sections. In addition, highly frequent types such as CARDINAL and ORDINAL act as background noise, co-occurring with almost everything and masking more informative relations, such as the link between a director and an actor. In this respect, the case of \"The Times of India\" is interesting, because it only emerges in the list of most frequent pairs, suggesting it is tied to specific stereotypical contexts like box-office reports for Indian films, rather than being uniformly significant across the corpus.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "To obtain meaningful results, several strategies should be adopted instead of this raw analysis.\n",
        "First, it is mandatory to filter out uninformative entity types (in particular, the CARDINAL and ORDINAL ones) to focus on semantically rich categories, like PERSON, ORG, and GPE. This will allow us to extract a meaningful knowledge graph (*e.g.* with actor-director relationships). Also, a more informative strategy would be to restrict co-occurrences to smaller textual windows such as the sentence level or a fixed-size context window, to increase the probability that two entities are actually related. Finally, distinguishing between article sections would be a powerful improvement, as it would allow us to separate narrative relations (with characters co-occurring in the plot) from production-related ones (where a studio and a distributor are co-occurring in a distribution section).\n",
        "\n",
        "To conclude this section, we can say that we tried to perform information extraction with NER, but that our retrieved data are in fact really noisy for this task, and that motivates the need for more specific methods. In order to be concise, we will not implement the proposed approach in this question, but they could be considered as future work."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ag0hSj3PtF8R"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "---"
