    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "syPKLu9KZ-il"
      },
      "source": [
        "#4.Text embeddings: semantic vectorization of the articles"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Our previous analyses have shown that NER-based representations suffer from substantial noise and are strongly influenced by generic editorial patterns, which limits their usefulness for genre-oriented clustering. To overcome this issues, we therefore turn to text embeddings in this section, which are likely to yield more informative clusters than the entity-based approach.\n",
        "\n",
        "Fundamentally, an embedding is a translation of discrete data (such as words or sentences) into continuous vector representations of fixed size. Unlike simple bag-of-words approaches which rely on exact keyword matching, embeddings map text into a semantic vector space, that preserves semantic similarity, *i.e.* such that texts with similar meanings are mapped to points that are geometrically close to each other. Here, this transformation is useful, as it allows to quantify the \"distance\" between movie plots using mathematical metrics, which is required before applying any clustering method.\n",
        "\n",
        "To perfom text embeddings, and as seen during the course [1], today's modern NLP relies on pretrained language models that combine statistical learning with vast amounts of data. For this project, we rely on BERT (for *Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers*). Unlike autoregressive models like GPT, BERT is an encoder-only model (see more details in the Appendix, section A.1), that is pretrained (using masked language modeling) to learn strong bidirectional representations, capturing the nuance and context of a word based on its surroundings.\n",
        "\n",
        "To process the text, BERT uses WordPiece tokenization (which is a sub-word tokenization method based on statistical occurrence of sub-strings), which effectively handles rare words and reduces vocabulary size compared to whole-word approaches. However, because standard BERT produces an embedding for each token, we will here use the Sentence-BERT (SBERT) model in order to obtain a single fixed-size vector for an entire movie article, as described in the lab 3 and 4 guidelines. SBERT modifies the original BERT architecture by adding a pooling operation on top of the contextualized word embeddings, which derives a single semantic vector $u\\in \\mathrm{R}^d$, where $d$ is the dimension of the embedding space, representing the whole document.\n",
        "\n",
        "For our movie corpus, these embeddings should capture thematic similarities crucial for genre clustering (for instance, grouping together films that share similar narrative tone or themes even when their surface vocabulary differs).\n",
        "\n",
        "`15.` We provide below the code to load the SBERT model, and write a method that returns the text\n",
        "embeddings of the corpus. Here, a (lightweight) pre-trained SBERT model, 'all-MiniLM-L6-v2', is loaded, as it is optimized for speed and performance. This model maps our sentences to a 384 dimensional dense vector space [7].\n",
        "\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "9T0eudUGWwDW"
      }
    },
