    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "cJvfjawHXm5o"
      },
      "source": [
        "`16.` Compared to a simple average word-vector representation (as seen during the previous lab sessions), SBERT offers a fundamental improvement. Firstly, we saw that the average word-vector approach relies on static embeddings, where each token is mapped to a fixed vector regardless of its usage, and the document is then represented by the mean of its word vectors. The problem is that this approach treats the document as a \"bag-of-words\", ignoring the word order, and does not account for the fact that the meaning of a word is highly context-dependent (*i.e.* it does not account for polysemy). For example, the word \"star\" would have the same vector whether referring to something in the sky or to a famous actor. As a result, articles that share the same vocabulary but structure information differently can end up close in the space, while more subtile genre or style differences are poorly captured.\n",
        "\n",
        "In contrast, SBERT uses the Transformer's attention mechanism to generate contextualized embeddings, where the representation of a token dynamically changes based on its surrounding words. It is explicitly fine-tuned using a specific structure to ensure that semantically similar texts are mathematically close in the vector space. This yields contextualised representations where the same word has different embeddings depending on its context, and pooling operation is optimized specifically for similarity tasks instead of being a naive average of keywords. For our movie corpus, this typically allows us to bring together articles that describe similar themes, tones, or genres (*e.g.* two psychological thrillers) even if they share relatively little vocabulary, which is exactly what we want for genre-oriented clustering.\n",
        "\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "---"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "IAvEh2aYUucM"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "yAfN575VaB2W"
      },
      "source": [
        "#5.Entity based distance matrix using the Jaccard index"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "We will now test whether a simple entities-based metric computed on the NER entity sets, the Jaccard distance [8], can still capture meaningful film similarities. The Jaccard index (also called Intersection over Union) is mathematically defined, for any two sets $A$ and $B$, by:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "J(A,B):= \\frac{|A \\cap B|}{|A \\cup B|}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "The Jaccard distance is explicitely derived from this index:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "J_{\\delta}(A,B):= 1-J(A,B)= 1-\\frac{|A \\cap B|}{|A \\cup B|}=\\frac{|A \\Delta B|}{|A \\cup B|},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "where $\\Delta$ denotes the symetric difference, *i.e.* $A \\Delta B= |A \\cup B|-|A \\cap B|$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Because Jaccard distance is defined on sets, we first need to represent each document as the set of its named entities, so that we can easily compute Jaccard distances.\n",
        "\n",
        "According to our findings of `14.`, relying on all the raw entities would introduce bias (*e.g.* if we decide to keep \"2023\", \"one\" and so on). Therefore, in the implementation of the NER-based distance method below, we represent each document as a set of named entities, but we strictly filter out noisy types (specifically, we remove CARDINAL, ORDINAL, TIME, PERCENT, DATE, MONEY, and QUANTITY types) to focus on semantically relevant matches, between actors and directors for example.\n",
        "\n",
        "--\n",
        "*Note: To illustrate the impact of this noise, we also computed the distance matrix unfiltered entities, i.e. keeping all the types. The results are provided in Appendix A.2, showing how generic terms artificially inflated similarities before filtering.*"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "nl3mlVXnichP"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "3HLYulMnZ1ou",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "outputId": "2fea337a-a2dc-487f-8712-38f67e99691e"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Shape: (499, 499)\n",
            "Sample:\n",
            "[[0.        0.9787234 1.       ]\n",
            " [0.9787234 0.        1.       ]\n",
            " [1.        1.        0.       ]]\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "def create_ner_distance_matrix(df):\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    Create a distance matrix based on Jaccard distance of NER entities.\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    # We define the list of noisy entities,based on our previous findings\n",
        "    noise = {'CARDINAL', 'ORDINAL', 'DATE', 'TIME', 'PERCENT', 'MONEY', 'QUANTITY'}\n",
        "\n",
        "    def get_clean_set(ents_list): # We defined this method that keeps only the text if the label\n",
        "                                  # is not in noise\n",
        "        return {t.lower() for t, l in ents_list if l not in noise}\n",
        "\n",
        "    entity_sets = df[\"entities\"].apply(get_clean_set) # We apply the filter to create sets\n",
        "    n = len(entity_sets)\n",
        "    dist = np.zeros((n, n))\n",
        "\n",
        "    # Then, the method performs the omputation of the symmetric matrix\n",
        "    for i in range(n):\n",
        "        set_i = entity_sets.iloc[i]\n",
        "        # Of course, the distance to self is always 0, so we skip j=i\n",
        "        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n",
        "            set_j = entity_sets.iloc[j]\n",
        "            inter = len(set_i & set_j)\n",
        "            union = len(set_i | set_j)\n",
        "            jaccard_dist = 1.0 - (inter / union) if union > 0 else 1.0\n",
        "            dist[i, j] = jaccard_dist\n",
        "            dist[j, i] = jaccard_dist\n",
        "\n",
        "    return dist\n",
        "\n",
        "# Finally, we calculate the NER distance matrix on our corpus\n",
        "ner_distance = create_ner_distance_matrix(df)\n",
        "print(f\"Shape: {ner_distance.shape}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Sample:\\n{ner_distance[:3, :3]}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "We also calculate some statistical results coming from the NER matrix to perfom a quick analysis in question `18.`, and print the most overlapping entities in our corpus.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Li9xo5bGsWyR"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "upper_indices = np.triu_indices_from(ner_distance, k=1) # We exclude the diagonal, as the distance\n",
        "                                                        # between a movie and itself is always 0, which will\n",
        "                                                        #lower the mean\n",
        "off_diag_values = ner_distance[upper_indices]\n",
        "mean_dist = np.mean(off_diag_values)\n",
        "percent_ones = np.mean(off_diag_values == 1.0) * 100\n",
        "print(f\"Mean Jacard distance (without diagonal): {mean_dist:.3f}, Percentage of cells = 1: {percent_ones:.1f}%\")"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "2khrebKUb0AM",
        "outputId": "afb1173d-314f-46e6-9e38-c085a9a67811"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Mean Jacard distance (without diagonal): 0.994, Percentage of cells = 1: 72.5%\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# We analyze below the overlaps, i.e. what are the entities that creates links\n",
        "# We first uild a lookup dictionary to retrieve the type of an entity from its text\n",
        "type_lookup = {}\n",
        "noise = {'CARDINAL', 'ORDINAL', 'DATE', 'TIME', 'PERCENT', 'MONEY', 'QUANTITY'}\n",
        "\n",
        "for ents in df[\"entities\"]:\n",
        "    for text, label in ents:\n",
        "        if label not in noise:\n",
        "            type_lookup[text.lower()] = label\n",
        "\n",
        "# Then we count the types of entities responsible for the overlaps\n",
        "shared_type_counter = Counter()\n",
        "\n",
        "# We need to re-access the clean sets used for the matrix\n",
        "clean_sets = df[\"entities\"].apply(lambda ents: {t.lower() for t, l in ents if l not in noise}).tolist()\n",
        "n = len(clean_sets)\n",
        "\n",
        "for i in range(n):\n",
        "    for j in range(i + 1, n):\n",
        "        intersection = clean_sets[i] & clean_sets[j] # We find the intersection between two movies\n",
        "        if intersection:  # If there is an overlap, we count the types of the shared entities\n",
        "            for entity_text in intersection:\n",
        "                if entity_text in type_lookup: # Now, we retrieve the entity's type\n",
        "                    shared_type_counter[type_lookup[entity_text]] += 1\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\n Drivers of similarity - Top shared entity types\") # We print the entities that are most often\n",
        "                                                            # shared between movies\n",
        "for type_, count in shared_type_counter.most_common(5):\n",
        "    print(f\"{type_}: {count} shared occurrences\")"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "VkH0Tyc0nex_",
        "outputId": "79c147a6-2fd0-48f4-db0f-377a26823a4e"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "\n",
            " Drivers of similarity - Top shared entity types\n",
            "NORP: 22398 shared occurrences\n",
            "GPE: 12638 shared occurrences\n",
            "ORG: 9562 shared occurrences\n",
            "PERSON: 8439 shared occurrences\n",
            "WORK_OF_ART: 3768 shared occurrences\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`18.` Using a Jaccard distance on filtered named entities (*i.e.* once the types considered as noise are removed) offers a theoretically robust approach, as it focuses on discrete, interpretable items (*e.g.* people, organizations, or locations). Then, two articles sharing many such entities are likely to be strongly related (*e.g.* a movie from the same franchise, or with the same directors, which often produce movies in the same genres). According to the previous results, we can say that only 27.5% of pairs retain a connection. These remaining overlaps are often driven by GPE (Countries, *e.g.* \"United States\") or NORP (Nationalities, *e.g.* \"Indian\", \"American\"), which together account for more than 60% of the shared entities (35,000 occurrences combined). In contrast, PERSON entities (containing actors and directors) only represent the 4th most common link, lagging behind ORG (that includes production companies). This confirms that the Jaccard NER distance acts primarily as a \"national industry\" detector, grouping Bollywood films together, or Hollywood films together, rather than a content-based genre clustering tool. While useful for mapping production webs, it fails to capture thematic and lexical proximity between films of different origins.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "For the specific task of genre clustering, we saw in question `17.` that our results highlight indeed a major limitation. We observed a high sparsity, with an average distance of 0.994 and the fact that 72.5% of pairs have a distance of 1 (*i.e.* there is no overlap), which shows that this metric is extremely strict. More precisely, the NER-based matrix using the Jaccard distance suffer from the exact match problem: by removing the structural noise, we in fact removed the artificial connectors, that link movies using numbers, like the year, budget etc.. For instance, all the movies contain the cardinal entity \"2023\", but this does not give any information regarding their genre. While this is methodologically correct, it reveals that two movies of the same genre rarely share the same named entities.  \n",
        "\n",
        "For example, taking two different horror movies from 2O23, let's say Scream VI and Saw X. It is important to recall that the NER only focuses on proper names, not on the full lexicon (*i.e.* words that appears in both movie description, such as \"blood\", \"kill\", are not taken into account). Therefore, even if they share the same genre, they typically have completely different casts, but also different directors and locations. But, since the Jaccard index relies on the intersection of sets, if there is no common actor or studio, the intersection is empty. Consequently, these two horror movies are considered as maximally distant (the distance between them equal one), just as distant as a horror movie and a comedy would be. Furthermore, the inverse scheme can also appear, whith two movies from a completely opposite genre, but sharing the same actors for example.\n",
        "\n",
        "Moreover, the Jaccard index is sensitive to set size. In our case, \"blockbuster\" articles, with often an extensive casting lists, have a higher probability of random intersection with other movies than independent films with short summaries.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Hence, the filtered NER-distance can be seen as relevant for detecting production links (like sequels or remakes, even if we are aware that a remake of a movie is rarely released on the same year that the movie itself), but fails to capture thematic similarity. It ignores common nouns (*e.g.* \"ghost\", \"love\", \"police\") which are the actual markers of a genre. This analysis confirms the necessity of using the text embeddings from section 4 in order to perfom clustering, which capture semantic proximity beyond exact proper noun matching.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "iyD4iC3cKgpV"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "vQEmK3xCeBaF"
      },
      "source": [
        "#6.Clustering: an unsupervised genre recovery for our corpus"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Having generated two complementary representations of our corpus, with first the SBERT text embeddings from section 4, that encapsulates the semantic content of our articles, and the NER-based Jaccard distances in section 5, throught the discrete entities, we now aim to evaluate their respective ability to recover the latent structure of the movie genres. To do so, we perform an unsupervised learning task using the spectral clustering model.\n",
        "\n",
        "The objective is to restructure the movie corpus into coherent groups solely based on these representations, without using the labels. By comparing the resulting clusters to the ground-truth Wikipedia genre labels (using some specific metrics), we will determine which representation between semantic text or discrete entities better encodes the thematic structure of the films. We utilize the `SpectralClustering` class from sickit-learn, as it is capable of identifying clusters with complex non-linear geometries, making it suitable for high-dimensional text data.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "6IkwMk06jzn5"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`19.` The first thing to do is to choose the number of clusters, corresponding here to a number of genres that we want to identify. Indeed, in unsupervised learning, no labels are available for our target (here, our movie articles), and the clustering procedure should be able to put together the ones that are close given their associated features (here, the embeddings or the NER-based distance). Changing the number of clusters will obviously change the way that the clustering procedure groups the movies together, making this number an hyperparameter. In order to make this choice efficient, we can first look at the number of unique genres that appear in our corpus of documents."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "kM_1LQiad3iw"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# We look at the actual number of genres in our dataset\n",
        "\n",
        "nb_unique_genres = df[\"genres\"].explode().nunique()\n",
        "print(f\"Number of unique genres: {nb_unique_genres}\")\n",
        "df[\"genres\"]"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 476
        },
