    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "From the results in the above cell, we observe that, as hypothesized, a clear \"core\" of dominant genres is present in our corpus, with \"drama\" (234) and \"comedy\" (108) characterizing more than the half of our movie articles. They are followed by distinct categories (like \"thriller\" (79), \"action\" (63), \"documentary\" (52), and \"romantic\" (50)) which are also well represented. However, the frequency drops significantly after the top 8-10 categories. Genres like \"noir\", or \"western\" appear only once or twice in the entire corpus, making them statistically insignificant for a clustering task. As we said earlier, trying to assign a dedicated cluster to a genre represented by a single movie would essentially force the algorithm to overfit or create noise.\n",
        "\n",
        "Consequently, we observe a natural cutoff around the 8th position, where the counts remain substantial, before slowly falling. Therefore, in order to capture the main thematic archetypes without diluting the results with rare labels, we set the number of clusters to 8. This choice covers the vast majority of the dataset's variance while grouping semantically overlapping tail-genres (*e.g.* \"crime\" often overlaps with \"thriller\") into these broader categories.\n",
