    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "We can first analyze the bottom plot, which represents the clusters obtained using the NER-based distance matrix. By looking at the t-SNE projection, a striking disconnection appears. Indeed, instead of a continuous distribution, the data is split into two completely separated groups (with a large green group on the left and a dense blue group on the right), with very few points in between identified to other clusters. This visualization confirms our \"sparsity\" hypothesis from Q18 (where 73% of our movie articles have a Jaccard distance = 1). It is likely that these two disconnected islands represent distinct production ecosystems (*e.g.* \"Hollywood\" versus \"Bollywood\" movies), that share absolutely no named entities (*i.e.* no actors, but also no locations in common). Consequently, the spectral clustering algorithm was forced to assign clusters based on this connectivity gap rather than thematic content, resulting in the two big clusters being defined solely by their lack of intersection. However, this does not ensure that a movie from the \"green cluster\" shares nothing in terms of genres with a movie from the \"blue cluster\". We clearly see here the limitation of the NER-based distance, computed in section 5. We also notice a few tiny \"clusters\" (identified in purple, sky blue and orange), capturing outliers that share entities among themselves.\n",
        "\n",
        "In contrast, the t-SNE projection of SBERT embeddings clusters exhibits a much more structured organization, forming a single continuous manifold (*i.e.* a unified cloud). Within this cloud, we observe that the clusters form local neighborhoods rather than isolated groups. For instance, we can see a distinct purple region on the right and an orange region in the center. This representation suggests that SBERT captures the semantic continuum of movie genres: genres are not mutually exclusive, but fluid categories with smooth transitions (*e.g.* a movie can be both between a \"thriller\" and a \"drama\"). The fact that the clusters are grouping neighbors within this continuous space confirms that the text embeddings successfully encode thematic proximity.\n",
        "Moreover, we notice that the clusters are more balanced in size than in the second case, and some of them form well-defined groups, suggesting that the text embeddings capture meaningful variations in content and themes across the articles (e.g. different genres or narrative styles) through its semantic vectorization, taking into account the lexicon of our movie articles.\n",
        "\n",
