    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`21.` Now, we will compare our two approaches using two particular metrics, namely the Adjusted Rand Index (ARI) and the Normalized Mutual Information (NMI).\n",
        "\n",
        "*-- Note: Our explanation of these methods are given in the Appendix, section A.3.2*."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "HFlp8q3hhX8L"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "Vu1_fPefYmaN",
        "outputId": "a1f70867-542e-4183-fe3f-ac9aaebc304d"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "text_cluster: ARI = 0.019, NMI = 0.101\n",
            "entities_cluster: ARI = -0.004, NMI = 0.084\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "def ari_nmi_scores(df, cluster_col):\n",
        "    \"\"\" Calculate the ARI and NMI scores.\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    true_labels = df['genres']\n",
        "    predicted_labels = df[cluster_col]\n",
        "    ari_score = adjusted_rand_score(true_labels, predicted_labels)\n",
        "    nmi_score = normalized_mutual_info_score(true_labels, predicted_labels)\n",
        "    return ari_score, nmi_score\n",
        "# The 'genres' column contains lists, sowe simplify the ground truth by taking only the first genre\n",
        "# This is a strong simplification for multi-label data, but necessary for standard ARI/NMI metr\n",
        "df['genres'] = df['genres'].apply(lambda x: x[0] if x else 'No Genre')\n",
        "\n",
        "for col in [\"text_cluster\", \"entities_cluster\"]:\n",
        "    ari, nmi = ari_nmi_scores(df, cluster_col=col)\n",
        "    print(f\"{col}: ARI = {ari:.3f}, NMI = {nmi:.3f}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "We computed the ARI and NMI to compare our unsupervised clusters against the Wikipedia genre labels. The quantitative evaluation leads to a clear conclusion, that is the superiority of semantic embeddings, demonstrating that SBERT outperforms the NER-based approach.\n",
        "\n",
        "Indeed, SBERT achieves a positive ARI and the highest NMI (0.101), which confirms that the text embeddings capture a genuine semantic signal related to the movie themes. The \"continuous manifold\" observed in the t-SNE (see `20.`) corresponds to meaningful genre groupings, even if the boundaries are fuzzy.\n",
        "\n",
        "In contrast, the NER-based clustering achieves an ARI of -0.004, meaning that it is effectively uncorrelated with genre labels. This quantitative score validates our hypothesis that sharing actors or locations (through the NER) creates some \"production clusters\" that are orthogonal to genre classification (*e.g.* a \"comedy\" vs. a \"thriller\"). However, the relatively higher NMI score for NER (0.084) compared to its negative ARI suggests a subtle correlation bias. We thought about a potential \"production bias\", because it could be possible that specific production ecosystems have dominant genre preferences. For example, the \"Bollywood\" cluster (identified by shared Indian entities) may heavily feature \"drama\" movies, while a \"Hollywood\" cluster might produce more \"action\" movies. Then, the NER-based clustering captures some genre information indirectly, not because it understands the content, but because it detects the regional industry which happens to specialize in those genres. In that sense, we could say that the NER-based approach is \"lucky\".\n",
        "\n",
        "We can also notice that, even if SBERT performs better, the absolute scores are low for both of the approaches. This can be explained by a bias inherent to our methodology: indeed, we compared the clusters against the single primary genre (*i.e.* the first tag), whereas movies are inherently multi-label.\n",
        "Hence, a movie tagged ['action', 'sci-fi'] might be validly clustered with other \"sci-fi\" movies based on its plot. However, if the primary label used for evaluation is \"action\", the metric counts this as an error. Consequently, the low ARI does not necessarily mean the clustering is bad, but rather that the unsupervised structure of the corpus is richer and more complex than the simplified single-label ground truth used for evaluation.\n",
        "\n",
        "Finally, despite the penalty of the single-label metric, the NMI score confirms that SBERT embeddings provide a significantly better proxy for content and genre than discrete entity matching."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "BeX0e8wzoFkN"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
