        "Looking at the SBERT heatmap, we can say that it shows a much richer and coherent specialization, even though \"drama\" remains prevalent (again due to the corpus distribution). Indeed, SBERT successfully identifies thematic niches, such as the 6th cluster, which is clearly an \"action/thriller\" group, because it has the highest concentration of \"action\" (0.36) and \"thriller\" (0.14), significantly higher than any other cluster. Other clusters can be highlighted, such as the 7th cluster, which seems to be the \"documentary\" group (0.33), separating real life from fiction, or cluster 0, that appears to be \"comedy/drama\" (respectively 0.36 and 0.15).\n",
        "Overall, this heatmap accurately reflects the multi-label nature of our movie articles. The SBERT embeddings capture the fact that an \"action\" movie often contains \"crime\" (0.11 for both in cluster 5)elements for example, whereas NER sees them as disjoint sets if the actors differ.\n",
