    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`22.` Several aspects of our experimental setup limit the quality of the classification, and suggest straightforward ways to improve it.\n",
        "\n",
        "First, the genre labels themselves are noisy and only partially informative. Many films have multiple genres, and some categories such as “drama” or “comedy” are extremely broad, while others are rare and under-represented. In our evaluation, we collapsed multi-label information into a single \"main genre\" (the first one), which inevitably loses nuance and penalises cases where a film sits between two genres (*e.g.* \"comedy-drama\", or \"thriller-horror\"). A more faithful evaluation would treat this as a multi-label problem, or group highly correlated genres into coarser but more balanced categories.\n",
        "\n",
        "Second, the text content of the Wikipedia articles mixes several heterogeneous sources of information (like plot summary, production details, box office, etc.). Genre is mainly reflected in the plot and sometimes in the first sentence, whereas production and reception sections introduce a lot of vocabulary that is weakly related to genre but strongly shared across films (names of countries, media, streaming platforms). Focusing the representation on the most relevant parts of the article (*i.e. *on the lead section and plot) or weighting sections differently could therefore improve genre separability.\n",
        "\n",
        "Third, the NER-based representation proved to be fragile despite our filtering efforts. Although we removed noisy types like CARDINAL or DATE in section 5, the remaining entities (GPE, PERSON) still introduced a \"production bias\". Common entities like \"United States\" or \"Hollywood\" appear in too many articles, acting as generic connectors that group movies by country rather than genre. A major improvement would be to apply a TF-IDF weighting [1] to the named entities to penalize these terms and focus on rare entities (like specific actors or directors) that truly define a style.\n",
        "\n",
        "On the text-embedding side, SBERT already provides a much richer approach, as suggested by the t-SNE plots and the higher ARI/NMI scores. However, we used the raw article text with a generic pre-trained model. Further improvements could come from domain adaptation (fine-tuning SBERT on a large corpus of film-related texts, in order to adapt the last layer to our task, as explained in the course [1]) or from incorporating additional supervision, for instance training a classifier to predict genres and then clustering in the induced feature space. We could also experiment with alternative document representations discussed in the course [1] (*e.g.* TF-IDF, LSI/LDA) to better capture topic-level structure.\n",
        "\n",
        "Also, the clustering algorithm itself is a pragmatic choice rather than an optimal one. Spectral clustering with a fixed number of clusters and default nearest-neighbour affinity is sensitive to hyperparameters and to the local density of the embedding space. Exploring other clustering methods (*e.g.* Gaussian Mixture Models, or density-based methods such as (H)DBSCAN) and tuning the number of clusters based on intrinsic criteria could better exploit the structure of the SBERT space and yield clusters that align more closely with genre, or reveal alternative groupings such as production country or franchise membership.\n",
        "\n",
        "Finally, the temporal restriction of the dataset constitutes a significant limitation. A single year fails to capture the diversity of cinema, and genres like \"western\" might be virtually absent in a modern snapshot like 2023, while others might be over-represented due to temporary trends. Expanding the corpus to multiple years would not only correct this temporal bias but also increase the sample size, providing the unsupervised algorithms with more data to identify robust genre patterns. Of course, we must be aware of the fact that the performance of clustering methods could be lower as the sample size increases.\n",
        "\n",
        "Overall, the results illustrate that both the nature of the data and the choice of representation have a strong impact on clustering quality. SBERT captures meaningful semantic structure but is constrained by imperfect labels, while the NER-based distance is largely dominated by named artefacts and requires more careful feature engineering to become competitive."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "D6cGs4VnKPXs"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "---"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "A1Shx8vtI9y-"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# - Conclusion -\n",
        "\n",
        "During this project, we investigated a complete NLP pipeline, from data collection via Wikipedia API (499 articles of films from 2023) to genre proxy-labeling, exploratory analysis, NER extraction, text embeddings (SBERT vs bag-of-entities), and spectral clustering evaluation (ARI/NMI).\n",
        "\n",
        "Given the noisy nature of Wikipedia film articles, we filtered entity types to isolate semantically meaningful NER relationships and reveal genuine film similarities. This approach proved relevant as the clustering results remained coherent. The formed clusters corresponded to logical genre groupings (*e.g.* comedy-drama). However, the ARI and NMI values were lower than those obtained with SBERT embeddings. Although these values are slightly biased due to using only the first genre of each film, it is reasonable to conclude that SBERT achieved superior performance.\n",
        "\n",
        "Moreover, the SBERT embedding-based clustering appears more homogeneous and distinct, whereas NER entity clustering primarily separates the corpus into broad production ecosystems. Thus, while NER successfully links films sharing specific actors or locations, it appears less suitable for identifying thematic groupings or narrative genres. The coherence observed in the NER heatmap analysis is therefore likely attributable to a \"production bias\" rather than genuine genre similarity.\n",
        "\n",
        "To conclude, while we could consider potential improvements, our results demonstrate that both the nature of the data and the choice of representation  have a strong impact on clustering quality.\n",
        "\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "GtjfPEI80xCF"
      }
    },
