      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# - Appendix -\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "AxPuDZkzxO6v"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## A.1. Comprehensive details on BERT and SBERT models\n",
        "\n",
        "BERT is based on a Transformer architecture and we saw during the course [1] that it relies on Masked Language Modeling (MLM): during training, the model randomly hides specific tokens and attempts to predict them using the surrounding context (both left and right simultaneously).\n",
        "\n",
        "However, standard BERT outputs embeddings for every single word, which is not practical for comparing whole documents. SBERT (Sentence-BERT) adapts this architecture using Siamese Networks (two identical BERTs sharing weights) combined with a pooling layer. This structure allows the model to condense an entire movie plot into a single fixed-size vector, making it computationally efficient to calculate cosine similarities between films.\n",
        "\n",
        "Here is an illustration of the SBERT architecture for a classification task. ![sbert.webp](data:image/webp;base64,)\n",
        "Figure - The SBERT architecture (Source: [towards data science](https://towardsdatascience.com/sbert-deb3d4aef8a4/))"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "HIaUszYpMJqA"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## A.2. Raw Jaccard Matrix for NER embeddings\n",
        "\n",
        "To validate the filtering strategy applied in `17.`, we computed the Jaccard distance on the full, unfiltered set of named entities. The comparison with the filtered approach yields a useful insight into the nature of NER noise.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "SrNJKbz2MQXf"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "def create_raw_ner_distance_matrix(df):\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    (Appendix version) Create a distance matrix based on all NER entities\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "\n",
        "    # Here, we keeps all entity texts, regardless of type\n",
        "    def get_raw_set(ents_list):\n",
        "        return {t.lower() for t, l in ents_list}\n",
        "\n",
        "    # We apply without filtering types\n",
        "    entity_sets = df[\"entities\"].apply(get_raw_set)\n",
        "\n",
        "    n = len(entity_sets)\n",
        "    dist = np.zeros((n, n))\n",
        "\n",
        "    for i in range(n):\n",
        "        set_i = entity_sets.iloc[i]\n",
        "        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n",
        "            set_j = entity_sets.iloc[j]\n",
        "\n",
        "            inter = len(set_i & set_j)\n",
        "            union = len(set_i | set_j)\n",
        "\n",
        "            jaccard_dist = 1.0 - (inter / union) if union > 0 else 1.0\n",
        "\n",
        "            dist[i, j] = jaccard_dist\n",
        "            dist[j, i] = jaccard_dist\n",
        "\n",
        "    return dist\n",
        "\n",
        "raw_ner_distance = create_raw_ner_distance_matrix(df)\n",
        "\n",
        "# We then compute statistics for comparison\n",
        "upper_indices = np.triu_indices_from(raw_ner_distance, k=1)\n",
        "raw_values = raw_ner_distance[upper_indices]\n",
        "\n",
        "raw_mean = np.mean(raw_values)\n",
        "raw_zeros = np.mean(raw_values == 1.0) * 100\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Mean distance: {raw_mean:.3f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"% of paires without intersection: {raw_zeros:.1f}%\")"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "nHAKuuOUeXJ-",
        "outputId": "71e51397-5278-4fc6-e2a0-45736a2913b7"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Mean distance: 0.980\n",
            "% of paires without intersection: 3.2%\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Recall that, in the filtered case of `17.`, 73.3% of pairs had no intersection (*i.e.* had a Jaccard distance = 1). Here, only 3.2% of pairs have no intersection. Hence, at first glance, the raw matrix appears much richer, as almost every movie is connected to every other movie. However, the high average distance (0.980) indicates that these connections are extremely weak. This massive drop in sparsity is not due to better semantic matching, but to artificial inflation caused by high-frequency structural noise. Entities like DATE (\"2023\") or CARDINAL (\"one\", \"two\") act as \"universal bridges,\" linking totally unrelated films simply because they share the same release year or budget. This confirms by a quantitative justification the use of filtering implemented in our main methodology."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ILhpne6ffBqB"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "##A.3. More on spectral clustering"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "GDVDSrFIeZ1Y"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### A.3.1 Changing the number of clusters\n",
        "\n",
        "We illustrate the sentitivity of the clustering procedure to the choice of the number of clusters on the SBERT approach.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "pLQdYmXJMG7_"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "n_values = [4, 8, 15]\n",
        "\n",
        "tsne = TSNE(n_components=2, perplexity=30, random_state=42, init='pca', learning_rate='auto')\n",
        "X_tsne = tsne.fit_transform(text_embeddings)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(18, 5))\n",
        "\n",
        "for i, k in enumerate(n_values):\n",
        "    labels = spectral_cluster(text_embeddings, n_clusters=k, features=True)\n",
        "    plt.subplot(1, 3, i+1)\n",
        "    plt.scatter(X_tsne[:, 0], X_tsne[:, 1], c=labels, cmap='tab20', s=20, alpha=0.8)\n",
        "    plt.title(f\"SBERT Clustering with k={k}\", fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\n",
        "    plt.xlabel(\"t-SNE Dim 1\")\n",
        "    plt.ylabel(\"t-SNE Dim 2\")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "nc4I9LoL0eji",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 269
        },
        "outputId": "ca80945a-baf1-470a-e1a8-4805b6f28046"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "display_data",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "<Figure size 1800x500 with 3 Axes>"
            ],
            "image/png": "\n"
          },
          "metadata": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The figure above illustrates the sensitivity of spectral clustering to the number of clusters k on SBERT embeddings. At k=4, the partition suffers from under-segmentation, merging distinct semantic regions into massive super-clusters that lack the necessary granularity for genre recommendation. Conversely, at k=15, we observe clear signs of fragmentation, where continuous semantic clouds are arbitrarily sliced into smaller pieces, suggesting overfitting to specific plot details rather than general archetypes. Therefore, k=8 appears to offer the optimal trade-off, as it respects the visual topology of the t-SNE manifold by isolating distinct peripheral islands while coherently dividing the central dense cloud, confirming our data-driven choice from `19`."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "HUhx7UMQZZNU"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### A.3.2 On the metrics and methods used during the clustering\n",
        "\n",
        "- About **t-SNE**: It is important to recall that t-SNE is a visualization tool, not a clustering algorithm. It preserves local structure (*i.e.* neighbors stay close) but often distorts global distances (the distance between two far-away clusters in the plot does not strictly represent their mathematical distance). However, the striking difference in topology between the two plots from section 6 (NER vs. SBERT) is a robust finding that validates our preference for text embeddings.\n",
        "\n",
        "- The **Adjusted Rand Index (ARI)**: The ARI measures the similarity between two data clusterings by counting pairs of samples that are assigned in the same or different clusters in the predicted and true partitions. It is the \"adjusted\" version of the Rand Index, ensuring that a random assignment yields a score close to 0.0. It is defined as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ARI = \\frac{RI - E[RI]}{\\max(RI) - E[RI]},$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with $RI$ the unadjusted Rand Index [11] and $E[RI]$ is the expected index under random permutation. A score of $1.0$ indicates a perfect match, while $0.0$ indicates random labeling.\n",
        "\n",
        "- The **Normalized Mutual Information (NMI)**:\n",
        "NMI is a normalization of the Mutual Information (MI) score to scale the results between 0 (no mutual information) and 1 (perfect correlation). It measures the reduction in entropy of class labels given the cluster labels, quantifying how much information the clustering shares with the ground truth. Its expression is:\n",
        "$$NMI(Y, C) = \\frac{2 \\cdot I(Y; C)}{H(Y) + H(C)},$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with $Y$ are the true labels, $C$ the clusters, $H(\\cdot)$ is the entropy, and $I(Y; C)$ is the mutual information between the two partitions.\n",
        "\n",
        "Remark: Since both ARI and NMI are mathematically defined for strict partitions (*i.e.* for one label per object), we simplified our evaluation by using only the primary genre of each movie in `21.`. This inevitably lowers the scores but allows for a standard comparison.\n",
        "\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "NAHffZ-Dpsep"
      }
    }
